## Why This Is Asked
Tests deep knowledge of bit manipulation techniques for combinatorial enumeration without branching.

## Key Concepts
- Next higher number with same popcount (snoob algorithm)
- Handling 64-bit overflow with masking
- Pure bitwise operations and shifts only
- Edge cases are constant-time checks

## Code Example
```javascript
function nextPermutation64(v) {
  const MASK = (1n << 64n) - 1n;
  if (v === 0n || v === MASK) return 0n;
  const c = v & -v;
  const ripple = v + c;
  const ones = v ^ ripple;
  const next = ripple | ((ones >> 2n) / c);
  return next & MASK;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of non-trivial bit interleaving without loops, a common low-level optimization in graphics/encoding.

## Key Concepts
- Bit spreading (Morton order)
- Mask-and-shift interleaving
- 128-bit composition from two 64-bit halves

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudocode outline (no branches)
uint128 interleaveBits128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  uint64_t lo = x.lo, hi = x.hi;
  uint64_t sLo = spreadBy2(lo); // using masks 0x5555.., 0x3333.., etc.
  uint64_t sHi = spreadBy2(hi);
  r.lo = sLo;
  r.hi = sHi << 1;
  return r;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to scale classic SWAR nibble-swaps to 128-bit values, ensuring implementations stay constant-time and border-safe.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit lane-wise bit-twiddling
- SWAR-style mask-and-shift patterns
- Safe handling of hi/lo halves

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 nibbleSwap128(uint128 x) {
  const uint64_t M = 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = ((x.lo & M) << 4) | ((x.lo >> 4) & M);
  r.hi = ((x.hi & M) << 4) | ((x.hi >> 4) & M);
  return r;
}
```

